# ==============================================================================================
# pins.env - EVERY version / digest / option pin used by llama-native-tools, on all platforms.
# Edit here only. linux/build.sh and linux/container-build.sh source this file as a shell
# script; macos/build-common.sh sources it too; windows/build-common.ps1 parses it. Every
# build echoes the resolved values at the top of its log, so a log always says what it used.
#
# It lives in linux/ because that is where the container build mounts and sources it; the
# other two platforms reach it as ../linux/pins.env. One file, one place to edit.
# ==============================================================================================

# --- Container images (Linux builds only) -----------------------------------------------------
# The manylinux images exist to fix the glibc baseline. Building on a modern desktop distro
# bakes THAT distro's glibc version into the .so, and glibc symbol versioning is forward-only,
# so the library would then refuse to load on anything older - a failure that only shows up on
# a user's machine.
#
#   image                                  glibc  runs on
#   manylinux_2_28_x86_64                  2.28   Debian 10+, Ubuntu 20.04+, RHEL 8+
#   manylinux_2_28_aarch64                 2.28   every 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS, Ubuntu 20.04+
#   manylinux_2_39_riscv64                 2.39   Ubuntu 24.04+, Debian 13+ (in practice every
#                                                 riscv64 distro is newer than this)
#
# These are the same three images, at the same dated tag and the same digests, that
# CodeBrix.VideoPlayback.Dav1d and CodeBrix.Audio pin. The digest is what the build actually
# pins: the dated tag is there so a human can see which image generation it is, but the FROM
# line in every Containerfile resolves to the digest, so an upstream retag cannot silently
# change the toolchain under a rebuild. gcc 14 in all three - llama.cpp needs C++17.
IMAGE_X64=quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_28_x86_64:2026.07.19-1
IMAGE_X64_DIGEST=sha256:a61875a2f84cab7df8de222ff12cabc08ff86eb4ad402ac90ba7bdaed9600cca
IMAGE_ARM64=quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_28_aarch64:2026.07.19-1
IMAGE_ARM64_DIGEST=sha256:162c81dfd3efc710732a571717d3c916a6945ebf279e879ddee3243af96fe46f
IMAGE_RISCV64=quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_39_riscv64:2026.07.19-1
IMAGE_RISCV64_DIGEST=sha256:04b3acc69a6cebb10ac1252587d202acce6445d91c783d3f92229bb2b2483069

# The derived images this tooling builds ON TOP of those (base + cmake + ninja). Building one
# is the documented "install the tools on the build machine" step and is the ONLY step that
# uses the network; the actual library build then runs with --network none.
DERIVED_IMAGE_PREFIX=codebrix-llama-build

# --- Build tools ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cmake and ninja as PyPI wheels, latest available on 2026-09-15, pinned exactly. They are
# installed into the derived Linux images from the manylinux image's own CPython
# (PYTHON_IN_IMAGE). On macOS and Windows the same two tools are installed on the machine
# (Homebrew / pip / winget); the scripts print the version actually used and BUILD-INFO.txt
# records it, so a newer tool is never invisible. llama.cpp needs cmake >= 3.14; the wrapper
# project needs >= 3.24 (for $<LINK_LIBRARY:WHOLE_ARCHIVE,...>).
CMAKE_VERSION=4.4.3
NINJA_VERSION=1.13.2
PYTHON_IN_IMAGE=/opt/python/cp313-cp313/bin/python

# --- Vendored source (in-repo; nothing is fetched at build time) --------------------------------
LLAMA_DIR=llama.cpp
LLAMA_COMMIT=815a2a5915f22ce6a760c676389c5dfe8535c08f
LLAMA_TAG=b10221
LLAMA_BUILD_NUMBER=10221
GGML_VERSION=0.18.0

# --- The shipped library ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE shared library per RID, package-unique name (matches CodeBrix.Audio's codebrix_miniaudio):
#   Linux    libcodebrix_llama.so      macOS    libcodebrix_llama.dylib     Windows  codebrix_llama.dll
# A plain "llama" name would collide with LLamaSharp's llama.dll and with a stock Ollama on a
# user's PATH. The managed side declares LibraryImport("codebrix_llama").
LIBRARY_BASENAME=codebrix_llama

# --- llama.cpp / ggml CMake options (the pinned build configuration, every platform) -----------
#   CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release            -O3, NDEBUG
#   BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF               llama and every ggml piece become STATIC archives; the
#                                       wrapper project (../wrapper/CMakeLists.txt) links them
#                                       whole-archive into the one shared library we ship.
#                                       NOTE: GGML_STATIC is NOT this - it only adds a bare
#                                       "-static" link flag and breaks every backend link.
#   CMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON  the static objects end up inside a shared library
#   GGML_NATIVE=OFF                     no -march=native: a portable baseline, not this CPU's
#   GGML_OPENMP=OFF                     ggml's own thread pool; OpenMP would add a libgomp /
#                                       vcomp dependency outside the allowed system-only list
#   GGML_CPU_KLEIDIAI=OFF               upstream default, stated explicitly: ON would make CMake
#                                       DOWNLOAD Arm's KleidiAI kernels (the only fetch in the tree)
#   GGML_BACKEND_DL=OFF                 backends are compiled in, not loaded from separate files
#   GGML_AVX512=OFF                     (x64) the floor is AVX2 - see the x64 line below
#   LLAMA_OPENSSL=OFF, LLAMA_CURL=OFF   no HTTPS client; would drag in OpenSSL / libcurl
#   LLAMA_BUILD_COMMON/TOOLS/EXAMPLES/TESTS/APP/SERVER=OFF
#                                       only the library; the conformance and smoke tools are
#                                       this folder's own C programs
LLAMA_CMAKE_OPTIONS="-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON -DGGML_NATIVE=OFF -DGGML_OPENMP=OFF -DGGML_CPU_KLEIDIAI=OFF -DGGML_BACKEND_DL=OFF -DGGML_AVX512=OFF -DLLAMA_OPENSSL=OFF -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_COMMON=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_APP=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_SERVER=OFF"

# --- CPU baselines per architecture -------------------------------------------------------------
# x64 (win-x64, linux-x64, osx-x64): AVX2 + FMA + F16C + BMI2 - what GGML_NATIVE=OFF gives on
#   x86 by default (Jeremy, 2026-09-15: "we don't want to support any pre-AVX2 x64 processors").
#   Haswell (2013) and everything since. AVX-512 is deliberately OFF: not universal (Intel
#   dropped it from consumer parts from 12th gen), and an AVX-512 floor would exclude most
#   current Intel desktops.
LLAMA_CMAKE_OPTIONS_X64=""
# arm64 (linux-arm64, win-arm64): armv8.2-a with dotprod and fp16 vector arithmetic. Those two
#   features are what make quantized matmul fast on ARM and are compile-time in ggml (no
#   runtime dispatch), so the floor is Cortex-A75 / Neoverse-N1 (Graviton2) / every Apple
#   Silicon and Snapdragon X part - 2018 and later. It EXCLUDES Raspberry Pi 4 (Cortex-A72,
#   armv8.0) and older. Raise or lower here; it is one flag.
LLAMA_CMAKE_OPTIONS_ARM64="-DGGML_CPU_ARM_ARCH=armv8.2-a+dotprod+fp16"
# osx-arm64: Apple Silicon is all armv8.4-a or newer with dotprod/fp16/i8mm; the same 8.2 floor is
#   used for one fewer thing to reason about, and Metal does the heavy lifting on that RID anyway.
LLAMA_CMAKE_OPTIONS_OSX_ARM64="-DGGML_CPU_ARM_ARCH=armv8.2-a+dotprod+fp16 -DGGML_METAL=ON -DGGML_METAL_EMBED_LIBRARY=ON -DGGML_BLAS=ON -DGGML_BLAS_VENDOR=Apple"
# osx-x64: CPU only (Jeremy, 2026-09-15). Metal OFF drops the Metal/MetalKit/Foundation
#   dependencies and the 19-second shader compile the Intel UHD 630 needed at first load.
LLAMA_CMAKE_OPTIONS_OSX_X64="-DGGML_METAL=OFF -DGGML_BLAS=ON -DGGML_BLAS_VENDOR=Apple"
# riscv64 (linux-riscv64): plain rv64gc. ggml's RISC-V vector (RVV) kernels are a COMPILE-TIME
#   choice with no runtime fallback, so a library built with them crashes on a board without
#   the V extension. The safe floor is scalar. If every target board is known to have RVV 1.0,
#   set GGML_RVV=ON (and the Z* extensions) here - and say so in BUILD-PROVENANCE.txt.
LLAMA_CMAKE_OPTIONS_RISCV64="-DGGML_RVV=OFF -DGGML_RV_ZFH=OFF -DGGML_RV_ZVFH=OFF -DGGML_RV_ZICBOP=OFF -DGGML_RV_ZIHINTPAUSE=OFF"

# --- Verification gate --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The highest GLIBC_x.y symbol version the shipped .so may reference. This IS the oldest system
# the binary can load on, so it is checked, not merely reported.
GLIBC_MAX_X64=2.28
GLIBC_MAX_ARM64=2.28
GLIBC_MAX_RISCV64=2.39

# Shared libraries the Linux .so is allowed to depend on. Anything else is a gate failure: it
# would mean the package demands a library be installed on the user's machine. libstdc++ and
# libgcc_s are deliberately ABSENT: the wrapper links them statically (-static-libstdc++
# -static-libgcc) so the C++ runtime inside libcodebrix_llama.so never depends on the host's.
ALLOWED_DEPS="libc.so.6 libm.so.6 libpthread.so.0 libdl.so.2 librt.so.1 ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 ld-linux-aarch64.so.1 ld-linux-riscv64-lp64d.so.1"

# The minimum macOS the dylibs will load on (both slices; checked by the gate, not just requested).
# 13.3 (Ventura), one floor for both slices. It was 11.0 until 2026-09-15, when the first
# osx-arm64 run showed that 11.0 was never a floor anyone could run on: ggml calls Accelerate
# through its NEW LAPACK interface (ACCELERATE_NEW_LAPACK + ACCELERATE_LAPACK_ILP64, set by
# ggml-blas/CMakeLists.txt for the Apple vendor), whose cblas_sgemm$NEWLAPACK$ILP64 symbol only
# exists from macOS 13.3. With an 11.0 target the linker made it a WEAK import - the dylib
# loaded on 11.0-13.2 and then crashed on a null pointer at the first BLAS matmul. ggml-metal
# likewise calls -[MTLSharedEvent waitUntilSignaledValue:timeoutMS:], macOS 12.0+. 13.3 is the
# smallest version at which every symbol the library references is real; the wrapper now compiles
# with -Werror=unguarded-availability-new so any future use of an API newer than this fails the
# BUILD rather than a user's machine. (ggml-metal's macOS-15 residency sets stay correctly guarded
# by @available.) Current .NET releases require macOS 13 or newer, so nothing is lost.
MACOS_MIN_VERSION=13.3

# The conformance assets (relative to llama-native-tools/). See test-vectors/README.txt.
TEST_VECTOR_DIR=test-vectors
TEST_VECTOR_MODEL=test-vectors/codebrix-conformance-tiny.gguf
TEST_VECTOR_EXPECTED=test-vectors/EXPECTED.txt
# The committed model's size and sha256. Every build regenerates the model with the library it
# just built and the result must be BYTE-IDENTICAL to the committed file - that proves the gguf
# writer in this build and the committed asset agree. If generate-test-model.c is ever changed on
# purpose, regenerate the file, update these two values AND re-establish EXPECTED.txt.
TEST_VECTOR_MODEL_SIZE=101056
TEST_VECTOR_MODEL_SHA256=66c90c32aea69570911279c49ca1252e60408a2fe2218000cff54444cdb6e7d9
# Absolute tolerance on every logit. The model is tiny and F32 throughout; CPU vs Metal vs
# NEON vs AVX2 differ in the last few bits (about 1e-6), so 1e-3 is three orders of margin
# while still catching a wrong kernel outright.
CONFORMANCE_TOLERANCE=0.001
